  v1: pH 5.747 · I = 0.0269 mol/kgw
  v1: copper inert: copper sits above zinc in the activity series (E° +0.342 V against -0.762 V), so the electrons would have to flow uphill: the less reactive metal does not displace the more reactive one
  v1: copper not corroding: copper sits above hydrogen in the activity series (E° +0.342 V), so in aerated neutral water it is the cathode of any corrosion cell rather than the anode: oxygen takes electrons at its surface and it stays as the metal. The green on an old copper contact is not rust and is not this reaction: it is a patina of basic copper carbonate and sulfate, grown over years from carbon dioxide and sulfur dioxide in the air on a first film of Cu2O. That is atmospheric weathering, it needs a gas phase this bench does not carry, and no route here claims it
